The Nautilus DevOps team is presently immersed in data migrations, transferring data from on-premise storage systems to AWS S3 buckets. 
They have recently received some data that they intend to copy to one of the S3 buckets.

S3 bucket named datacenter-cp-828356713 already exists. Copy the file /tmp/datacenter.txt to s3 bucket datacenter-cp-828356713 using Terraform. 
The Terraform working directory is /home/bob/terraform. Update the main.tf file (do not create a separate .tf file) to accomplish this task.
Note: Right-click under the EXPLORER section in VS Code and select Open in Integrated Terminal to launch the terminal.
Main.tf
resource "aws_s3_bucket" "my_bucket" {
  bucket = "datacenter-cp-828356713"
  acl    = "private"

  tags = {
    Name        = "datacenter-cp-828356713"
  }
}
SOLUTION
Here is your updated /home/bob/terraform/main.tf to copy /tmp/datacenter.txt into the existing S3 bucket:

hcl
resource "aws_s3_bucket" "my_bucket" {
  bucket = "datacenter-cp-828356713"

  tags = {
    Name = "datacenter-cp-828356713"
  }
}

resource "aws_s3_object" "datacenter_file" {
  bucket = aws_s3_bucket.my_bucket.id
  key    = "datacenter.txt"
  source = "/tmp/datacenter.txt"
  etag   = filemd5("/tmp/datacenter.txt")
}
💡 Key Details
aws_s3_object: This is the resource used to upload a local file to S3. The source argument points to the local file path.

key: The object name inside the bucket. Setting it to datacenter.txt uploads the file with that name.

etag = filemd5(...): Ensures Terraform detects changes if the local file is modified, and re-uploads when needed.

Removed acl = "private": This argument is not supported in AWS provider v5 (which your provider.tf uses). Buckets are private by default, so no ACL is needed.

🚀 Execution Steps
bash
cd /home/bob/terraform
terraform init
terraform plan
terraform apply --auto-approve
✅ Confirm No Changes
bash
terraform plan
It should return:

text
No changes. Your infrastructure matches the configuration.
🔍 Verify (optional)
bash
aws --endpoint-url=http://aws:4566 s3 ls s3://datacenter-cp-828356713/
You should see:

text
2026-11-18 12:00:00        <size> datacenter.txt
You can also confirm the content matches:

bash
aws --endpoint-url=http://aws:4566 s3 cp s3://datacenter-cp-828356713/datacenter.txt - | diff - /tmp/datacenter.txt && echo "File matches"
⚠️ If the Bucket Is Not in Terraform State
If terraform apply tries to recreate the bucket (because it exists in AWS but not in Terraform state), import it first:

bash
terraform import aws_s3_bucket.my_bucket datacenter-cp-828356713
Then run terraform apply again. This ensures Terraform manages the existing bucket instead of trying to create a duplicate.


